00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.310
OK, so then let's come back.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.530
I spend-- this is the chapter--

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:59.170
I spent the most time, in
terms of per chapter so far.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.860
We're really talking
about the transport law.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.240
And the content that
we talk to is best

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.700
applied to ballistic transport,
meaning there is no scatter.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.330
You can still
treat with scatter.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.580
But I say, right now,
what we're saying

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.180
is if I want to know the
transport between any two

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.780
points, and now
the transmittance.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.950
So we learned how to calculate
transmittance of interface,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.440
and how transmittance
of film, multilayer,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.530
vacuums, tunneling--
all those, in theory,

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:36.850
now you can at
least say you're not

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.800
afraid of dealing with
these type of problems.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.440
So if the transmittance and
the left flux, whatever flux.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.270
Here I'm doing the heat flux.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.260
So h bar omega is energy.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:02:00.340
And so I'm doing 1
to 2 minus 2 to 1.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:05.260
But at the end, apply the
principle of detailed balance.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.060
Which is essentially second law.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.010
It says at an equilibrium,
the lead flux must be zero.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.970
And otherwise you have
created an imbalance.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:22.690
Heat can go spontaneously
from form hot and cold sides.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.070
So that's not valid and
that's the principle

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.200
of detailed balance.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.430
So essentially, you can say,
when we want to calculate flux,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:36.110
is the velocity, transmittance,
the quantity that

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.130
are transported here
is what mode, right,

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:44.740
quantum state, and
then the distribution

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.480
function difference.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.490
It states how many
particles we have

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:55.060
either corresponding
temperature, chemical

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.050
potential, and of
course, the energy.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.220
So the detailed
balance really saved us

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:07.510
from calculating both
sides because let's say

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.860
you can take any of the sides
and do your calculation as

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.900
long as you know
the transmittance

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.880
from the starting side to the
other side or other point.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.880
And I emphasized at the
end of last lecture,

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.000
those kinds of transport,
there a high-low equilibrium.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.480
So it's actually
hard to really define

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.190
a quantity that we're used to.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:40.030
Even temperature,
chemical potential, those

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.770
are all thermodynamic
quantities.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:48.250
And the example we spent
some time to explain

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.040
is thermal boundary resistance.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.700
When I have acoustic wave
comes toward the interface,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.740
on this side this is--

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.040
in fact, this is my T1, the
corresponding distribution

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:09.850
of phonons coming from this side
and refracted and transmitted.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.682
And on the other side
I have the incoming

00:04:12.682 --> 00:04:14.140
with the characteristic
temperature

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.100
at T2 reflected and transmitted.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.240
So locally, I do not
have at the interface,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.710
on each side of interface,
I do not have equilibrium.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:30.160
And when I force my
temperature definition,

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.740
this temperature,
the local energy,

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.370
is not actually just a T1
characteristics, but also

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.550
transmission of T2
characteristics.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:44.470
So if you redefine
a local temperature,

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.770
or if you do charge
transfer, a local chemical

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.280
potential,
electrochemical potential,

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.410
it's not the incoming one.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.560
And you have to recalculate
what's the local--

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.780
for example, if I want
the first temperature

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.050
to be equivalent measure
of a local energy density,

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.150
I will define the
local temperature

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.470
based on local energy density.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.200
That gives me a different
temperature than this one.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.710
So those are [INAUDIBLE]
equilibrium process.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:16.000
I think the best
way you understand

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:23.140
this is taking the undergraduate
problem of blackbody radiation.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.580
If I have two black surfaces.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.590
And this is T1 and this is T2.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.980
And now if you
look at the inside,

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.700
I have photons come
in from 1 to 2.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.000
And this has a
characteristic temperature 1.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.030
And then I have photons
go in the other side

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.690
and have a characteristic
of temperature 2.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.930
And then you ask, what's
the photon temperature?

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:53.230
You say there's no
equilibrium, clearly.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:58.450
You can't define a photon
temperature inside.

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:04.870
And now you say, OK, imagine I
have a detector with two face.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.340
Both side is black.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:11.890
So I have a small detector
with both sides are black.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:14.350
On one side, it absorbs
the temperature of heat

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.180
from with the temperature T1.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:18.880
The other side was
the temperature T2.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.070
And you have an
equilibrium temperature

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.920
here because of the
absorber thermalize, right,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.670
those two temperatures,
two groups of photons.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:30.850
And that temperature
is a flat inside.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:32.420
It's because there's no change.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.690
It depends on going from
one side to the other.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.830
There's no change.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.830
So if you say, OK, my define--

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.830
the photon conductance, if
I define based on the photon

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.540
temperature here,
are the two sides,

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:49.690
you know there's no delta T and
your conductance are infinite.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.960
But if I define, based on
the temperature of the solid,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.040
I can measure this easily.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.180
I don't have that detector.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:06:59.560
But I can define--

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.960
if I put in my
thermocouple to measure

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:07.600
to solid surfaces, solid objects
to black body, I'll measure T1,

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.180
I'll measure T2.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.730
My thermocouple is not measuring
the photon temperature.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:17.580
my thermocouple is measuring
the solid temperature.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.510
In fact my thermocouple
is measuring the phonon

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:21.150
temperature.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.590
So you're actually
fooled in this case.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:27.410
But that's whether you-- in your
engineering calculation, that's

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.430
the definition you'll be using.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.580
So this is the temperature
you'll be using.

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.490
You're using the
solid temperature.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:39.140
So I gave another
example where say

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.490
people measure the
conductance of a wire,

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:45.530
electric conductance.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.520
They put the two metal
probe-- for example,

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.270
you do a carbon nanotube.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.330
You put a two big electrodes.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.040
You measure voltage here.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.820
That's like you're measuring
the two solid temperatures.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:03.500
You can't measure the electron
chemical potential exactly

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.270
at the contact.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.280
You don't have a
probe that easily

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.650
measure that local voltage.

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:12.660
If you measure that
local voltage, again,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.690
the electron in the
ballistic regime,

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.520
there is no voltage drop.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:20.460
You should get an
infinite conductance.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:25.000
So this definition dependent,
when you do your experiment,

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.540
you need to know what
they're comparing to.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:32.340
Don't compare oranges
with apples, OK?

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:38.669
So with this, basically
you can really,

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:41.880
if you do nanoscale
transport, this

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:43.570
could be a foundation
to understand

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.790
a lot of papers in literature.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:49.230
People are studying different
kinds of nanoscale transport

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.760
problems.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:55.270
We're talking about conductance
and many other examples.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:58.050
So just to take the
carbon nanotube.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:04.450
And if you write down let's say
the electrical conductivity,

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:08.400
so there is this a
universal conductance.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.500
And turns out that
universal conductance,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:21.450
for one-dimensional object, it
doesn't depend on the material.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:28.260
And the conductance G per mole,
per quantum channel, quantum

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:34.830
state, is a e squared h bar--

00:09:37.033 --> 00:09:38.700
let me check, what
are the other factor?

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:41.425
2?

00:09:41.425 --> 00:09:42.980
[INAUDIBLE] e squared, pi h bar.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.600
So if you substitute a Lambert,
this conductance or equivalent

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.860
resistance, which is the
inverse of the conductance

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:02.430
is 12,265 ohm.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:06.770
OK, so through each channel,
you add another channel,

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.350
you have two parallel channel.

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:14.420
They conductance doubles,
resistance reduced by half,

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.470
the quantum states.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.960
So people have, in the
past, they measure--

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:20.840
in fact, there
was the experiment

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:23.750
doing say various
quantum transport

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:26.690
particle at low temperatures.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:31.130
And this is electrical.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:33.800
And you can do thermal.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:37.910
If I have two temperature
difference between two points,

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:44.030
and the thermal conductance
for one-dimensional object

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:55.244
is pi kb squared T
and divide by 6 h bar.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:04.880
And this one-- so this
is, again, universal

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.190
because it doesn't have a
material property there.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:14.450
And if you plug in your Lambert
conductance here, I get about--

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:23.810
this 10 to the 12th times T and
the Watt meters squared Kelvin.

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:26.090
So this number will be
interesting for some

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:29.610
of my students doing the
interface resistance.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:33.530
We the--

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:36.260
Oh, this is a positive 12--

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.420
well, let me see.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:46.910
Negative 12-- wait, I
have some number problem.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:55.980
Pi kb T square, kb 46, 10 to
the minus 12 T and Watt meter

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:57.590
squared.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:04.010
So the-- no I think I
have to check this number.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:08.060
It doesn't look right because
G typically is 10 to the 9th,

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:09.600
this is minus 12.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.370
So maybe I'll check.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:15.410
I don't know where
it's wrong now.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:19.370
OK so that's the
universal conductance.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:26.180
And if we do, for example, we do
near field, I have two plates.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:30.770
And I have typically,
the wave comes in,

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:34.730
and it has reflection,
and has transmission.

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:36.350
We said that when
the two surfaces are

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.650
brought the close by, you
have [INAUDIBLE] phenomena.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:45.560
And again, with that, you
can calculate transmittance.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.560
And you do your integration
over all states,

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:53.330
you can get the increase
of the heat transfer

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:54.920
due to the tunneling.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:58.070
Or if you're interested in
charge, the electrical charge

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:01.050
tunneling, all those
can be done now.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:05.670
So I think it will be--

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.880
I encourage now, you
can look at literature.

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:13.850
People study all those different
nanostructure, conductance,

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:14.750
transport.

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:19.550
And this is very
often discussed.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:24.420
What I have not finished
is what's the velocity.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.860
It turns out this
one is actually a one

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:31.430
that we ask at the beginning,
how fast the particle really

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:33.690
travels.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:42.080
So let's discuss this
velocity, group velocity.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:50.840
And this is a concept
sometimes very

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:54.920
hard to appreciate,
to understand.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:58.100
What is a group velocity?

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:01.550
So definition wise, it
would be relatively easy.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:04.010
But why we see
the group velocity

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.260
is typically when we use--

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.350
when we calculate
the charge, the heat,

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:16.400
that's the velocity I do in
my v, vx here, for example.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:18.120
Why I use group velocity?

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:24.650
So first, let me take a
picture of phonon dispersion

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:30.540
where in specific direction
I have k, I have omega,

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:34.460
and I have acoustic
phonon, one branch only.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:35.870
I'm looking at it.

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:39.980
And the group velocity is
tangent of every point.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.430
And that's the group velocity.

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:47.030
And it's this velocity
we use when we

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:49.790
stop into the energy transport.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:54.890
That's how fast--
how much energy say

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:59.960
a phonon would carry at that
specific mode, frequency.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:03.860
Why I use that velocity?

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.590
First let's look at
a wave, because I've

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:10.970
been talking about plane
wave representation, right?

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:15.890
So a wave that I usually
use, either vector or scalar.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:19.130
Now I just write a
simple scalar wave

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:24.950
is a minus i omega t minus kx.

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.950
I'm assuming that k is in
the x direction, simple.

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:32.970
And this is the plane wave
representation I have.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:37.860
What's the origin of my time--

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:41.850
was the end of the time
here for two plane wave?

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.450
That's minus infinite
to plus infinite.

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:51.730
That's a true plane wave.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:52.990
There's no ending point.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:16:00.530
Any time I have
an ending point, I

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:05.120
don't just have one
single frequency there.

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:15.260
So for example, if I
have a periodic signal,

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:17.690
I can say this is omega 0.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:19.940
But only has a finite
time span, right?

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.900
Let's say t0.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.950
It only has a finite time span.

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:32.040
And normally you say, OK,
I have a periodic signal.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:37.990
But mathematically,
that's not quite correct,

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:40.630
because this signal does
not go from minus infinite

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:42.820
to plus infinite.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:50.140
And if I have periodic signal or
only segment of time or length,

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:54.220
I can do Fourier series.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:57.760
So you can do your
Fourier analysis.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:01.480
And you see the real
frequency of this wave

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.000
is not-- a let's
say here the omega

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:10.599
0 is my fundamental
frequency of this wave.

00:17:10.599 --> 00:17:12.460
And then I have omega 0.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:13.900
I have harmonics.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:16.599
Omega 0 will be
my highest modes.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:18.819
And the 2 0, and the--

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:25.119
so different the frequency
around the central frequency.

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:29.750
That's why I do the
Fourier analysis.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:36.290
OK, so now, because of this,
because in a solid or whatever,

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:41.330
there's never really a wave
that go from minus infinite

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:43.610
to plus infinite.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:48.470
The generators, for example,
the atomic vibration or electron

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:51.650
transition from high
energy to low energy,

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:55.040
they only spend a
certain amount of time.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:58.710
Or the event only spends
a certain amount of time.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:02.600
So I have to
consider what happens

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:08.690
when the actual event is,
for example, happening

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:09.560
at a short time.

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:16.070
And in this case, I have
multiple frequencies, not just

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:19.160
