speed, new speed there.

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:26.240
And this speed in the limit
of very narrow of delta omega

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:29.150
delta k, I have
derivative, right?

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:37.790
So this equals d omega dk and
this is my Vg, group velocity.

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:43.590
That's why I'm doing
the group velocity.

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:52.040
And when I do transport, I
use V, the group velocity.

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:58.880
The phase velocity
is omega over k.

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:01.220
That's this triangle here.

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:02.990
That's the phase velocity.

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:06.200
And in fact, if you go to
check your density of states,

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:07.810
when you calculate
density of states,

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:10.790
you want phase velocity.

00:24:10.790 --> 00:24:15.470
You want both phase
velocity and group velocity.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:18.260
OK, but so when I
do transport-- when

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:21.500
I think about how fast the
particle or energy is going

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.590
from one place to
other, the wave packet,

00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:27.710
and I should use
the group velocity

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:31.800
for the actual transport speed.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:35.060
And when I calculate it,
when I convert these modes

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:37.910
into density of states,
I should be careful,

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:43.760
some is the group velocity,
some is the phase velocity.

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:47.000
And of course, this is
the only one direction.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:51.260
And the three directions, in
general, the group velocity

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:53.120
is a vector.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:57.830
So this one is, for
example, if you do Cartesian

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:08.822
coordinate for the k space is d
omega dk x kx and d omega dk y

00:25:08.822 --> 00:25:15.410
ky and D omega dk z kz.

00:25:15.410 --> 00:25:20.390
So that's a general
group velocity.

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:22.730
And with that, you can say--

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:29.780
now if I go back
to phonons, and I

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:34.040
have the optical
phonon, acoustic phonon.

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:37.820
If you look at the group
velocity, very small k.

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:41.810
This one, the tangent
here, is much larger.

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:45.560
Then at the end of the k,
zone boundary, k [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:48.740
is 0, flat.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:53.450
So here is a speed of sound
that they can measure.

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:57.350
And here is zone boundary
group velocity for 0.

00:25:57.350 --> 00:26:01.240
So the acoustic phonon
group velocity is changing.

00:26:01.240 --> 00:26:04.220
Optical phonon,
the group velocity

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:06.740
is typically very
small, even though they

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:08.000
phase velocity is very high.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:11.120
Omega over k, omega
is very large.

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:14.270
Phase velocity is
large is large.

00:26:14.270 --> 00:26:18.150
But group velocity
is very small.

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:21.140
And when you do
specific heat, it

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:25.730
turns out the simple Debye model
or plus Einstein model is fine.

00:26:25.730 --> 00:26:29.660
But when you do
transport, this velocity

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:31.830
becomes more important.

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:35.580
So even though most models
in literature, and in fact,

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:39.020
90% of those papers you
check now, you'll notice,

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:42.995
we typically simplify the
Debye model or Einstein model

00:26:42.995 --> 00:26:46.250
when we do heat transfer
thermal conductivity.

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:49.670
But that will typically lead
to very different results

00:26:49.670 --> 00:26:55.330
from reality because of the
use of the group velocity here.

00:26:55.330 --> 00:26:58.610
It's a major factor.

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:00.560
And this is the case of phonons.

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:03.230
And you can also--

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:09.180
so you think about electrons.

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:11.510
So for example, I have electron.

00:27:11.510 --> 00:27:12.960
This is the energy.

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.170
This is the k.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:18.710
That's typical dispersion.

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:25.850
And so the center, the minimum,
the group velocity is 0.

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:29.570
This is because it's
a parabolic bend.

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:31.560
We said that some
electron, in fact,

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:35.690
in the problem in the midterm,
