00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:47.090
we know that the speed of
light and media is c0 over n.

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:49.790
This is a phase velocity.

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:52.280
And it turns out that you
check the optical constant

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:55.460
of some media refractive
index, real part.

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:56.300
This is n.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:57.690
This is omega.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:30:02.030
And at some omega 0, you
may have-- this is a 1,

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:03.470
this is a 1--

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:05.705
and the sum of these
omega look like this--

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:13.480
refractive index, real part.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:21.870
So what happens is here,
of course, a less than 1.

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:26.280
So the phase velocity is much
larger than the speed of light.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:27.240
But that's fine.

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:31.500
You will say, OK, that's
not the energy velocity.

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:35.070
OK, so you're still comfortable.

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:44.010
But then if you go to take the
derivative of dn d say omega,

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:47.910
you'll find that even the
group velocity in this region

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:50.940
could be larger than 1.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:54.120
So you run into, are
you violating Einstein's

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:55.500
relativity principle?

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:04.600
And the reason then,
is that, in this case,

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:06.660
it's actually not
the group velocity,

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:10.410
