00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:17.040
over a certain spatial extent.

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:26.370
So this is a wave
packet composed

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:30.800
of a lot of frequencies.

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:42.020
And let's say there is the
frequency spread is delta nu

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:47.375
And in optics, there is
definition of coherence length.

00:37:00.110 --> 00:37:04.430
And in fact, there's coherence
is a very extensively discussed

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:05.520
in optics books.

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:07.020
If you want to
understand coherence,

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:08.870
you should read that.

00:37:08.870 --> 00:37:10.700
And there is a
quantum coherence.

00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:12.590
There's classical coherence.

00:37:12.590 --> 00:37:14.000
And there's spatial coherence.

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:16.980
There's time coherence.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.950
And here, I'm giving the
simplest, the time coherence.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:26.190
So if I have a wave packet
that has a frequency spread,

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:30.060
then there is also
corresponding space spread,

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:32.740
because they have propagated
in different velocity.

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:35.640
So this space spread
is coherent length.

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:42.360
This is of the order of c
delta nu, the speed of light

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.430
divided by the spread.

00:37:44.430 --> 00:37:46.830
That gives you the length scale.

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:59.250
So physically, this wave packet
also covers some length scale.

00:37:59.250 --> 00:38:01.920
And the space coherence
is at two points

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:07.350
whether the wave packet
are correlated or not.

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:10.200
And typically with a
random thermal source,

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:13.080
for example, I have vibration--
atomic vibration-- here

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:16.230
and another place,
I typically just

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:18.870
ignore this spatial coherence.

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:23.880
The two vibrations are
spatially uncorrelated.

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:28.110
But there are other cases where
they are actually correlated.

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:33.180
OK but say what I
actually have a lot

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:39.880
of misunderstanding myself
over this process, many years--

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:42.900
But first, let's say I
mentioned that-- if you recall--

00:38:42.900 --> 00:38:50.140
if I have a wave packet, if
I have a perfect interface,

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:54.190
and it comes through
the interface.

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:57.250
And some of this
was transmitted.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:39:01.690
And you can see that is still
the same packet transmitted,

00:39:01.690 --> 00:39:03.100
reflected.

00:39:03.100 --> 00:39:04.840
Some of this is reflected.

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:11.410
So at that 0 thickness
at the interface,

00:39:11.410 --> 00:39:15.130
the reflection and
incoming are spatial--

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:16.240
so they're correlated.

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:18.680
This is the same wave packet.

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:21.710
So you have to, if it's a
perfect interface there,

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:25.390
you have to use your wave
mechanics to deal with.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:29.770
It's a wave, right?

00:39:29.770 --> 00:39:32.620
So that's a single
simple interface.

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:37.440
Then what about a film?

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:47.730
So if I think about
a film of thickness d

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:54.360
and my wave packet
comes in, I'm looking at

00:39:54.360 --> 00:40:00.150
to treat them the same, because
if they are the same, if they

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:05.350
overlap spatially, you have to
include that phase addition.

00:40:05.350 --> 00:40:07.800
They interfere with each other.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:09.690
If they come from
different wave packet,

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:13.350
they are not correlated, because
the one that the immediate--

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:17.100
say in the next
time step may not

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:20.670
be correlated with the
one that are emitted

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:23.100
by the previous time step.

00:40:23.100 --> 00:40:26.880
So two different wave
packet, I don't care.

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.940
I consider the
one that somewhere

00:40:29.940 --> 00:40:33.660
coming from a source
propagating inside the film

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:36.240
and now reflected.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:39.660
So whether I consider
interference or not,

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:41.490
then this is the
same wave packet.

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:44.370
Maybe different amplitude, but
they come from the same parent.

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:51.410
So if they spatially
overlap, you

00:40:51.410 --> 00:40:53.420
have to include
the interference.

00:40:53.420 --> 00:40:55.910
You have to use the
wave superposition

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:58.410
that we did before.

00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:04.340
So one of the
criteria is if this d

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:09.880
is a much larger then
coherence length,

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:12.820
if this is very large, the
same wave packet-- of course

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:16.340
at the interface, I have to
use that Fresnel formula,

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:18.100
which is a wave result.

00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:20.440
But once they are
away from interframe,

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:25.580
most of the region in
the film, like a window,

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:27.830
this packet will not overlap.

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:32.030
So you can just trace them
as particles, trajectories.

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:34.370
Forget about the phase,
because you do phase,

00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:38.870
you'll get the same result.

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:40.400
So that's one scenario.

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:44.240
In fact, I thought that was
the most scenario when I was--

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:46.550
the first paper when I
was a student, I wrote--

00:41:46.550 --> 00:41:47.510
a PhD student--

00:41:47.510 --> 00:41:49.730
I wrote was on this problem.

00:41:49.730 --> 00:41:54.320
And I thought that would answer
the wave particle transition.

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:56.943
And in fact, in optics--

00:41:56.943 --> 00:41:57.860
so there are two ways.

00:41:57.860 --> 00:42:01.280
One is the transfer matrix
to calculate reflectance.

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:04.100
The other is just completely
ignore the interference

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:10.310
and do retracing energy
bounce back and forth.

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:12.780
But you look at the formula,
it's very different.

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:17.690
So I actually was just
a [INAUDIBLE] formula.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:20.840
And then my advisor say, oh,
you should look at theories.

00:42:20.840 --> 00:42:23.570
So I went to check it
turns out there is all

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:25.130
this optical coherence theory.

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:26.780
And I use the theory.

00:42:26.780 --> 00:42:29.280
And I say that's the transition.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:32.120
So this is where the
demarcation comes from.

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:33.890
Film is thick.

00:42:33.890 --> 00:42:36.710
You neglect to say inference--

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:38.030
film is thin.

00:42:38.030 --> 00:42:39.200
You don't need [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:42.830
It's all very logical.

00:42:42.830 --> 00:42:47.770
But then, years later, I
realized this is a problem.

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:50.360
Other people probably
know this very well.

00:42:50.360 --> 00:42:57.890
But the problem is if I have a
black body, which has a spread,

00:42:57.890 --> 00:43:00.520
and in fact, you can calculate
the effect of spread.

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:02.420
The black body has a--

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:03.830
it's opaque.

00:43:03.830 --> 00:43:05.940
You say 0 to infinite
of the frequency,

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:09.440
but they can calculate
effective bandwidth of that 0

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:12.260
to infinite because it
peaks around [INAUDIBLE]..

00:43:16.110 --> 00:43:19.880
But see when I calculate
the multilayer structure,

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:21.800
particularly
superlattice, we mentioned

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:24.500
before, Bragg reflector.

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:27.814
Bragg reflector is a 1-2
1-2 repeating structure.

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:38.850
And it turns out that you
really don't get this result.

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:40.620
The limit [INAUDIBLE]
are very thick.

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:43.380
You still get your
wave result. You

00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:46.650
can never get the result of
where you neglect completely

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:50.870
the interference effect.

00:43:50.870 --> 00:43:52.030
So I thought about this.

00:43:52.030 --> 00:43:58.000
And now the physical explanation
is, again, is the same parent--

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:00.390
the wave packet came
from the same parent

00:44:00.390 --> 00:44:02.410
that actually overlap.

00:44:02.410 --> 00:44:03.100
Why?

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:08.860
If you say, OK, here I
have a packet comes in.

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:12.220
And they go into
the first layer.

00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:17.080
And that packet
and reflect once,

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:23.110
refract to the second
layer, reflect once.

00:44:23.110 --> 00:44:28.240
This from the split from the
same parent packet there.

00:44:28.240 --> 00:44:29.980
Of course, because
of the time delay,

00:44:29.980 --> 00:44:34.810
I don't have overlap between
this one and this one,

00:44:34.810 --> 00:44:37.280
because this one already--

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:39.110
this one travel longer time.

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:43.720
So when this wave packet
coming into this layer,

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:46.860
this one already gone off.

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:51.490
However, but this one has
a lot of reflection here,

00:44:51.490 --> 00:44:55.450
another reflection,
back and that's

00:44:55.450 --> 00:44:58.060
coming from the same parent.

00:44:58.060 --> 00:45:01.360
And these two can
spatially overlap.

00:45:01.360 --> 00:45:04.720
And here, even though
you have very thick one,

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:08.080
they can still interfere
with each other.

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:14.590
So your structure actually
gave this packet a coherence.

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:21.670
So you cannot completely just
use the simple coherence length

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:24.070
to neglect the wave effect.

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:34.590
So that's say a lesson
learned in optics.

00:45:34.590 --> 00:45:39.870
And the next lesson is the
phonon scatter, which we'll

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:45.330
dive in in the next lecture.

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:48.840
And phonon-phonon
scatter-- so we'll

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:51.600
have the lattice vibration.

00:45:51.600 --> 00:45:56.230
And they are typically three
phonon interaction process.

00:45:56.230 --> 00:46:11.130
So in this case, what I
have is inelastic scatter.

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:15.470
So now you have to
look at the scatter.

00:46:15.470 --> 00:46:19.490
So inelastic scatter
is when I have

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:22.880
one phonon omega 1,
two phonon on comes in,

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.100
and they interact and
forms third phonon

00:46:25.100 --> 00:46:28.900
and the energy of the third
is the sum of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:46:32.540 --> 00:46:37.490
Those kind of process pops
up randomly in the solid,

00:46:37.490 --> 00:46:42.020
because the phonon propagate
anywhere they are not fixed.

00:46:42.020 --> 00:46:49.700
So those processes are usually
consider as a phase destroying.

00:46:49.700 --> 00:47:05.680
So inelastic
scattering-- typically,

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:11.890
if your process is dominated
by inelastic scattering,

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:17.560
and that gives you the,
let's say, the mu free path,

00:47:17.560 --> 00:47:22.300
the distance between
the scattering event.

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:26.170
And of course, the
scattering itself,

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:32.620
will [INAUDIBLE] this
phase superposition.

00:47:32.620 --> 00:47:35.080
But between one point and
to the other, the wave,

00:47:35.080 --> 00:47:38.590
once they scatter, it
propagates as a packet.

00:47:38.590 --> 00:47:41.710
The left system is scattered
to another direction

00:47:41.710 --> 00:47:44.470
as a completely
different wave packet,

00:47:44.470 --> 00:47:49.100
because they lose their
parent information.

00:47:49.100 --> 00:47:51.260
It's random.

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:55.480
So in this case, I can
consider the waves--

00:47:55.480 --> 00:47:59.140
I can forget about
the superposition

00:47:59.140 --> 00:48:03.760
of waves between
different scatter waves.

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:08.700
So I trade them as particles,
uncorrelated particles.

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:11.150
So inelastic scattering is--

00:48:11.150 --> 00:48:16.490
typically you can
trade as a particle,

00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:19.430
if this process is dominant.

00:48:19.430 --> 00:48:21.030
But there's another process.

00:48:21.030 --> 00:48:27.380
For example, if I have
a solid or impurity atom

00:48:27.380 --> 00:48:30.540
in the material, and
the wave comes in,

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:33.080
whether it's an electron
wave or a phonon wave.

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:35.960
This comes in and another
omega, it goes out,

00:48:35.960 --> 00:48:38.900
and the same one goes.

00:48:38.900 --> 00:48:43.210
They just change
direction, right?

00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:45.790
This is a light, for
example, typically when you

00:48:45.790 --> 00:48:52.970
have dust in the sky, the light
comes in, refract to your eye.

00:48:52.970 --> 00:48:56.295
This kind of process, the
particle position is fixed.

00:48:59.180 --> 00:49:02.960
But the scattering direction
depends on the solution.

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:06.950
You have to solve
the Maxwell equation

00:49:06.950 --> 00:49:10.640
for that problem in a class
they call [INAUDIBLE] theory.

00:49:10.640 --> 00:49:18.410
Or you solve the electron,
atom, all those problems because

00:49:18.410 --> 00:49:19.940
of this guy is fixed.

00:49:19.940 --> 00:49:24.110
So the scatter one actually
have a fixed phase relation.

00:49:24.110 --> 00:49:27.950
Different direction, you
have different fixed wave.

00:49:27.950 --> 00:49:29.840
So these are the
elastic scatter.

00:49:34.220 --> 00:49:37.520
And typically, elastic
scattering really

00:49:37.520 --> 00:49:40.400
does not destroy phase,
because let's say

00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:41.660
this position is fixed.

00:49:41.660 --> 00:49:43.700
It's just got
different direction

00:49:43.700 --> 00:49:45.410
made a different phase.

00:49:45.410 --> 00:49:51.120
But this one, this one has
a fixed phase relationship.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:57.990
So then the question is, do
I treat them as particles

00:49:57.990 --> 00:50:00.630
if this one is dominant?

00:50:00.630 --> 00:50:03.300
Turns out that here, you've
got really complicated

00:50:03.300 --> 00:50:04.870
possibilities.

00:50:04.870 --> 00:50:09.510
For example, you
have many particles.

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:15.060
And you can have a wave comes
in, and say scatter into here

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:17.490
and scatter into here.

00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:20.310
And you could also
have waves scatter back

00:50:20.310 --> 00:50:22.920
along the same paths
and completely cancel

00:50:22.920 --> 00:50:23.670
the incoming wave.

00:50:26.220 --> 00:50:28.490
So those kind of
wave superposition

00:50:28.490 --> 00:50:32.670
is called localization.

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:37.830
So if it's an elastic scatter,
you can to have localization.

00:50:45.630 --> 00:50:51.480
And you could also have where,
because at least each one

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:54.360
you can have complete
pattern of scattering even

00:50:54.360 --> 00:50:57.990
though the different directions
are fixed with regarding

00:50:57.990 --> 00:50:59.550
to the incoming one.

00:50:59.550 --> 00:51:02.610
But when you consider
many of them,

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:04.860
spatially, they
are random enough

00:51:04.860 --> 00:51:10.080
you actually can neglect
those phase superposition.

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:13.380
So in that case,
you can go again

00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:14.790
into the particle picture.

00:51:14.790 --> 00:51:17.520
If it's a localization,
you cannot.

00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:22.080
But in the outer limit,
you can neglect the phase,

00:51:22.080 --> 00:51:25.990
just treat them as particles.

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:31.110
OK here, you will
say when I can do it.

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:33.060
I can't tell you.

00:51:33.060 --> 00:51:34.450
It's very complicated.

00:51:37.000 --> 00:51:40.360
And the idea of if
you actually read,

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:43.450
because we discussed several
times, the photonic crystal.

00:51:46.300 --> 00:51:49.720
And the photonic crystal,
that concept the name

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:52.120
was coined by Yablonovitch.

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:54.940
But in the same year,
there was another paper

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:57.910
studying the same phenomenon.

00:51:57.910 --> 00:52:02.260
That's by John Sajeev.

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:04.480
And he was studying
the same structure.

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:08.915
And he wants to look at when
the wave gets localized.

00:52:08.915 --> 00:52:10.540
So they're a completely
different angle

00:52:10.540 --> 00:52:12.020
looking at the same problem.

00:52:12.020 --> 00:52:13.990
So yeah, photonic
crystal is actually

00:52:13.990 --> 00:52:16.360
typically accredited to
two people, Yablonovitch

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:17.530
and John Sajeev.

00:52:22.020 --> 00:52:24.510
So you look at the
different literature,

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:28.920
it turns out in optics, people
talk a lot of coherence.

00:52:28.920 --> 00:52:32.490
And of course, the electron
transport, a lot of people

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:33.570
also talk of coherence.

00:52:33.570 --> 00:52:39.470
And one of the biggest problem
with the quantum computing

00:52:39.470 --> 00:52:41.990
is how you can keep
the wave phase.

00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:43.160
You don't lose the phase.

00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:47.420
You lose the phase, your
superposition of waves is gone.

00:52:47.420 --> 00:52:53.850
So to maintain the
face coherence time,

00:52:53.850 --> 00:52:56.810
in electric-- if you
read the literature,

00:52:56.810 --> 00:52:58.530
there are many
different lengths.

00:52:58.530 --> 00:53:00.140
For example, there is this--

00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:05.500
I think it's a very
Thouless length--

00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:06.500
different length scales.

00:53:10.470 --> 00:53:14.310
And of course, is there are
diffusion lengths, all those.

00:53:14.310 --> 00:53:17.490
So talking about those
transition picture, very, very

00:53:17.490 --> 00:53:18.370
complicated.

00:53:18.370 --> 00:53:24.210
And we have been studying heat
conduction in superlattice.

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:30.000
And recently, we think we
have really found evidence

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:32.220
that there is a phase
coherence contribution

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:34.680
to thermal conductivity.

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:39.390
And there is another
very mysterious result

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:41.850
on thermal conductivity
of nanowires,

00:53:41.850 --> 00:53:47.070
where there are some rough
nanowire people reporting

00:53:47.070 --> 00:53:51.300
way below what the classical
size effect could predict.

00:53:51.300 --> 00:53:55.110
And another paper being
written and some speculation

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:59.370
where the localization
is the reason or not.

00:53:59.370 --> 00:54:01.930
But there is really
no strong proof.

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:04.590
So doing theory,
doing experiments

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:11.300
in all those regimes
are very difficult.

00:54:11.300 --> 00:54:14.630
So given what I said--

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:19.190
so you see, this is an area
that needs more research.

00:54:19.190 --> 00:54:21.530
You might be
interested in doing it.

