But the most complicated
is here, the interaction.

01:16:33.150 --> 01:16:36.770
And I will discuss this
in the next lecture.

01:16:36.770 --> 01:16:38.780
That's a very complex term.

01:16:38.780 --> 01:16:44.990
But what I want to drop to
you is the Boltzmann equation

01:16:44.990 --> 01:16:50.660
is based on, if one particle
distribution, the assumption,

01:16:50.660 --> 01:16:52.655
if one part
distribution function,

01:16:52.655 --> 01:16:57.750
you know all the other
particle distribution function.

01:16:57.750 --> 01:17:03.180
And the reason is this
molecular chaos assumption.

01:17:03.180 --> 01:17:17.630
So molecular chaos-- and
just to say the particles

01:17:17.630 --> 01:17:22.370
do not really interfere
with each other.

01:17:22.370 --> 01:17:23.790
They scatter, they interact.

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:27.200
But you know the distribution
function of one particle,

01:17:27.200 --> 01:17:29.580
then you know all
the N particles.

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:32.540
So this is the N particle
distribution function.

01:17:32.540 --> 01:17:38.360
t rn pn, that's all that we
want to write in the Liouville

01:17:38.360 --> 01:17:39.460
equation

01:17:39.460 --> 01:17:46.700
And molecular chaos is just
this is the f1 one t r1 p1

01:17:46.700 --> 01:17:56.670
times the same t1 t rn pn.

01:17:59.300 --> 01:18:01.700
So if I know this
distribution function

01:18:01.700 --> 01:18:05.630
as a general function
of space momentum,

01:18:05.630 --> 01:18:11.460
I know the all the N particle
distribution function.

01:18:11.460 --> 01:18:12.890
So this is the--

01:18:12.890 --> 01:18:15.260
it's an independent.

01:18:15.260 --> 01:18:17.000
So you do have collision.

01:18:17.000 --> 01:18:20.570
But after collision they
forgot where they came from.

01:18:20.570 --> 01:18:22.940
The lost their memory.

01:18:22.940 --> 01:18:24.470
It's molecular chaos.

01:18:24.470 --> 01:18:27.920
That's a fundamental
in how you can

01:18:27.920 --> 01:18:32.360
get from the Liouville
theory equation

01:18:32.360 --> 01:18:33.740
to the Boltzmann equation.

01:18:36.400 --> 01:18:42.190
OK, and just one more
comment, for example,

01:18:42.190 --> 01:18:46.330
Boltzmann equation, when
you have dense fluids,

01:18:46.330 --> 01:18:47.780
it doesn't apply.

01:18:47.780 --> 01:18:50.710
So what people have
tried to modify

01:18:50.710 --> 01:18:53.000
is actually do 2
particles and consider

01:18:53.000 --> 01:18:54.955
final size of the molecule.

01:18:54.955 --> 01:18:56.830
There's another equation
called the Anscombe,

01:18:56.830 --> 01:18:59.710
which is a two-particle
distribution function.

01:18:59.710 --> 01:19:03.070
But the equation became
very hard to handle.

01:19:03.070 --> 01:19:05.740
Not many people ever solved it.

01:19:05.740 --> 01:19:10.300
And so that's since the '60s
more effort were actually going

01:19:10.300 --> 01:19:12.640
into the linear response,
because the computer became

01:19:12.640 --> 01:19:13.270
available.

01:19:13.270 --> 01:19:18.860
People can do computational
analysis and response theorem.

01:19:18.860 --> 01:19:20.410
OK.

